From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] shaders/colorspace: decode Dolby Vision with the exact matrix
 of its primaries

pl_shader_decode_color() decodes a Dolby Vision picture to BT.2020 RGB
through LMS: R'G'B' = ycc_to_rgb * (YCbCr - offset), the PQ EOTF, the
RPU's rgb_to_lms, a fixed lms2rgb, and the PQ OETF. That is the decoding
ETSI GS CCM 001 describes (clause 6.2.2): YCCtoRGB gives the non-linear
RGB (or LMS) of the HDR output picture, RGBtoLMS takes its linear RGB to
LMS, a space common to every source, and lms2rgb goes from that LMS to
BT.2020. RGBtoLMS thus carries the primaries of the picture: its default
there, [5845 9702 837; 2568 12256 1561; 0 679 15705] / 16384, is that of
P3-D65 RGB (lms2rgb times it is 1.3e-4 from the exact P3-D65 to BT.2020
matrix, and 0.13 from that of P3 with the DCI white).

For a picture whose RGB is BT.2020, as on a profile 7 or 8 stream, the
two matrices should cancel; for P3-D65 their product should be the exact
P3-D65 to BT.2020 matrix. They are not quite: the RPU rounds its
coefficients to 1/16384, and lms2rgb is the inverse of coefficients a
little apart from them (2.8/16384 on one, on the RPUs of a profile 7 FEL
stream): their product is off by up to 2e-4. Between the EOTF and the
OETF that moves light from the bright components of a pixel to the dark
ones, which PQ then shows as hundreds of codes: a component that should
be black next to a red at 4000 cd/m^2 gets about 0.5 cd/m^2, code 490 of
4095.

Where lms2rgb times the RPU's rgb_to_lms is within 1e-3 of the exact
matrix from the linear RGB of BT.2020, P3-D65 or BT.709 to BT.2020
(computed in double from libplacebo's primaries, for every picture),
that matrix is taken in place of the product. For BT.2020 it is the
identity: the round trip is left out and the signal only clamped to
[0, 1], as libplacebo's PQ EOTF clamps it (31f498ec, in v7.387.0; the
libplacebo-9931 of this tree before that), so the decoded picture is the
composed signal itself. The clause does not restrict the primaries
of the HDR output picture (it refers to the display primaries of SMPTE
ST 2086), and BT.709 is taken by the same rule. The three matrices are
0.13 and more apart, and a 14-bit rounding with the error of lms2rgb
stays within 2e-4 of its own. Any other matrix (the identity of a signal
whose YCCtoRGB gives LMS already, other primaries or another white such
as DCI-P3's, or one off by more) goes through LMS as before. A Dolby
Vision display does the same rounding in its own LMS, and next to the
bright component it is not seen there; it is not part of the signal,
which was graded in the RGB the RPU names, and an output of that signal
has no reason to carry it.

On all 2878 pictures of that stream (17.8 billion pixels of their active
areas, in 64-bit floats, with that clamp), the round trip moved 65 %
of the pixels off the exact one, a third of those by one code of 4095;
10 % of all the pixels by 37 codes or more, 1 % by 276 or more, up to
679, with either composer (their percentiles agree within a code). With
this patch the decoded picture is the exact one.

Measured on Mesa's software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), on top of
libplacebo-9930 and 9931, on pictures 44, 45, 47, 51, 704, 930 and 1509
of that stream with both composers: the picture is within one code of
4095 of the exact round trip (two in at most 137 pixels with the
reshaping). Before, it was off by up to 2 codes on picture 51, 12 on
44, 558 and 679 on 45 and 47, and on 704, 930 and 1509, where much of
the picture is bright, by up to 469 to 604 codes in 59 to 64 % of the
pixels, most of them by 4 codes or more. Made-up RPUs on the planes of
picture 51: the default rgb_to_lms of the clause and that of P3-D65
(both rounded to 1/16384) are decoded with the exact P3-D65 matrix, that
of BT.709 with BT.709's, each within one code of its exact round trip
(two in at most 72 pixels with the reshaping); the identity (an LMS
signal), P3 with the DCI white, and the stream's matrix with one
coefficient 40/16384 off stay on the general path, byte for byte as
before, with both composers.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later, that of libplacebo and of the file changed,
whose notice stays as it is.
---
 src/shaders/colorspace.c | 106 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 105 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/shaders/colorspace.c b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
index cfdb99d..a97c2da 100644
--- a/src/shaders/colorspace.c
+++ b/src/shaders/colorspace.c
@@ -332,6 +332,63 @@ void pl_shader_dovi_reshape(pl_shader sh, const struct pl_dovi_metadata *data)
 #endif
 }
 
+#ifdef PL_HAVE_DOVI
+// Hades: for the Dolby Vision decoding, the matrix from the linear RGB of
+// some primaries to that of BT.2020, in double (both of the white of D65)
+static double dovi_det3(const double m[3][3])
+{
+    return m[0][0] * (m[1][1] * m[2][2] - m[1][2] * m[2][1])
+         - m[0][1] * (m[1][0] * m[2][2] - m[1][2] * m[2][0])
+         + m[0][2] * (m[1][0] * m[2][1] - m[1][1] * m[2][0]);
+}
+
+// RGB to XYZ: the columns are the primaries, scaled so that RGB = 1 is the white
+static void dovi_rgb2xyz(const struct pl_raw_primaries *p, double m[3][3])
+{
+    const struct pl_cie_xy c[3] = { p->red, p->green, p->blue };
+    const double w[3] = { p->white.x / p->white.y, 1.0,
+                          (1.0 - p->white.x - p->white.y) / p->white.y };
+    double a[3][3];
+    for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) {
+        a[0][k] = c[k].x / c[k].y;
+        a[1][k] = 1.0;
+        a[2][k] = (1.0 - c[k].x - c[k].y) / c[k].y;
+    }
+    const double det = dovi_det3(a);
+    for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++) {
+        double b[3][3];
+        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
+                b[i][j] = j == k ? w[i] : a[i][j];
+        }
+        const double s = dovi_det3(b) / det; // Cramer: (a^-1 w)[k]
+        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
+            m[i][k] = a[i][k] * s;
+    }
+}
+
+static void dovi_to_bt2020(enum pl_color_primaries prim, double out[3][3])
+{
+    double src[3][3], dst[3][3], inv[3][3];
+    dovi_rgb2xyz(pl_raw_primaries_get(prim), src);
+    dovi_rgb2xyz(pl_raw_primaries_get(PL_COLOR_PRIM_BT_2020), dst);
+    const double det = dovi_det3(dst);
+    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
+            const int i1 = (j + 1) % 3, i2 = (j + 2) % 3, j1 = (i + 1) % 3, j2 = (i + 2) % 3;
+            inv[i][j] = (dst[i1][j1] * dst[i2][j2] - dst[i1][j2] * dst[i2][j1]) / det;
+        }
+    }
+    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+        for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
+            out[i][j] = 0.0;
+            for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++)
+                out[i][j] += inv[i][k] * src[k][j];
+        }
+    }
+}
+#endif
+
 void pl_shader_decode_color_ex(pl_shader sh,
                                const struct pl_color_decode_args *args)
 {
@@ -484,7 +541,54 @@ void pl_shader_decode_color_ex(pl_shader sh,
             { 0.01736321, -0.04725154,  1.03004253},
         }};
 
-        pl_matrix3x3_mul(&dovi_lms2rgb, &repr->dovi->linear);
+        // The RPU's rgb_to_lms is that of the RGB of the picture, whose
+        // primaries it names: through lms2rgb it is the matrix from that RGB
+        // to BT.2020. Rounded to 1/16384, and lms2rgb the inverse of
+        // coefficients a little apart (up to 2.8/16384 on a profile 7
+        // stream), the product is off by up to 1.8e-4, which moves light from
+        // the bright components of a pixel to the dark ones: hundreds of PQ
+        // codes near black. Where it is within 1e-3 of the exact matrix of
+        // BT.2020, P3-D65 (the primaries of the default rgb_to_lms of ETSI GS
+        // CCM 001) or BT.709, that matrix is taken, from the primaries, in
+        // double; for BT.2020 the round trip through LMS is the identity, and
+        // it is left out: the signal is only clamped, as the EOTF has it.
+        // These three are 0.13 and more apart. Any other matrix (the identity
+        // of a signal whose nonlinear matrix gives LMS already, or one of
+        // other primaries or white) goes through LMS as before.
+        double conv[3][3], exact[3][3];
+        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+            for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
+                conv[i][j] = 0.0;
+                for (int k = 0; k < 3; k++)
+                    conv[i][j] += (double) dovi_lms2rgb.m[i][k] * repr->dovi->linear.m[k][j];
+            }
+        }
+        static const enum pl_color_primaries dovi_prims[] = {
+            PL_COLOR_PRIM_BT_2020, PL_COLOR_PRIM_DISPLAY_P3, PL_COLOR_PRIM_BT_709,
+        };
+        int dovi_prim = -1;
+        for (int n = 0; n < PL_ARRAY_SIZE(dovi_prims) && dovi_prim < 0; n++) {
+            dovi_to_bt2020(dovi_prims[n], exact);
+            bool close_to = true;
+            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+                for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
+                    close_to &= fabs(conv[i][j] - exact[i][j]) < 1e-3;
+            }
+            if (close_to)
+                dovi_prim = n;
+        }
+        if (dovi_prim == 0) {
+            GLSL("color.rgb = clamp(color.rgb, 0.0, 1.0); \n");
+            break;
+        } else if (dovi_prim > 0) {
+            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+                for (int j = 0; j < 3; j++)
+                    dovi_lms2rgb.m[i][j] = exact[i][j];
+            }
+        } else {
+            pl_matrix3x3_mul(&dovi_lms2rgb, &repr->dovi->linear);
+        }
+
         ident_t mat = sh_var(sh, (struct pl_shader_var) {
             .var = pl_var_mat3("lms2rgb"),
             .data = PL_TRANSPOSE_3X3(dovi_lms2rgb.m),
